#   binary     anchor    open   first  second  first-second  chain_replays
#   pre-fix    lagging   476     324      36       284             1
#   pre-fix    current   360     320      36       285             1
#   post-fix   lagging   472     226      34       192             0
#   post-fix   current   357     322      38       286             1
#
# The three arms that still replay agree at 284/285/286 ms across two
# binaries and two anchor states; the one that does not is 192 ms.
#
#   SAVING  93.0 ms  (double difference against the three replaying arms)
#           91.5 ms  (directly against the positive control)
#
# Replicates O237's independently measured 88 ms at 102,001 audit records,
# by a different instrument, through a surface rather than a unit test.
#
# The open pays one too: an open whose anchor lags costs ~118 ms more than
# one whose anchor is current (476 vs 360 pre-fix, 472 vs 357 post-fix).
#
# NOT claimed: a curve. One configuration, one corpus size. 10^6 remains
# O237's measurement and is an extrapolation here. And nothing moves for an
# ordinary writable deployment, which anchors after every write and takes the
# open's short-circuit — the `current` rows are what that looks like.
